<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pomponius Mela</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">fl. c. 43 CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pomponius Mela was a Roman geographer from southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispania">Hispania</a>, the earliest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> author of a surviving geographical work. His De Chorographia, written around 43 CE, describes the coasts of the known world, with notes on peoples and places. It is an important source for peoples of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a>, such as the Ausci and Sordones.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pomponius-mela.jpg" alt="Pomponius Mela" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Américo Toledano · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Estatua_de_Pomponius_Mela_-_Ceuta_001_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Little is known of Pomponius Mela beyond his name and birthplace, the small town of Tingentera or Cingentera on Algeciras Bay in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, identified as Iulia Traducta. The text naming it is corrupt. He wrote under the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claudius">Claudius</a>: his allusion to a proposed British expedition by the reigning emperor is almost certainly to that of Claudius in 43 CE, and several references to events of the reign of Augustus, especially new names given to Spanish towns, show it cannot refer to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a>. Mela belonged to the Italic communities settled in Spain, like the two <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seneca-people">Senecas</a>, Lucan, Martial and Quintilian. It has been conjectured that he was related to Marcus Annaeus Mela, son of Seneca the Elder and father of Lucan.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>His short work, De situ orbis in three books, fills less than a hundred pages of ordinary print. Apart from the geographical parts of Pliny's Historia naturalis, which cites Mela as an important authority, it is the only formal treatise on the subject in Classical Latin. It mainly agrees with Greek writers from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eratosthenes">Eratosthenes</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a>. Mela describes the coasts in the manner of a periplus, probably because his material came from navigators. He begins at the Straits of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gibraltar">Gibraltar</a>, follows the south coast of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>, goes round <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> and returns to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, then follows the ocean coast from Spain and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> round to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and back.</p><p>He divides the Earth into five zones, of which only two are habitable, and uniquely among ancient geographers asserts that antichthones live in the southern temperate zone, cut off by the torrid zone. Like most classical geographers he regards the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caspian-sea">Caspian Sea</a> as an inlet of the Northern Ocean. His knowledge of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> was ahead of the Greek writers: he defines the coasts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bay-of-biscay">Bay of Biscay</a> more accurately than <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eratosthenes">Eratosthenes</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a>, and he is the first to name the Orcades, or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orkney">Orkney Islands</a>. He also speaks of a great northern bay, the Codanus sinus, with a large island called Codanovia.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The work remained in use nearly to the year 1500. The first printed edition appeared at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> in 1471, and the first critical edition was by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joachim-vadian">Joachim Vadian</a> in 1518. Johann Friedrich Gronovius edited it in 1685 and 1696, Arthur Golding's English translation appeared in 1585, and F. E. Romer published a recent English translation in 1998.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pomponius Mela” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Pomponius Mela</b> calls them the most distinguished of the Aquitanian peoples.</blockquote><a href="/state/ausci">Ausci</a></li><li><blockquote>Known only from Greek and Roman writers such as Avienus, <b>Pomponius Mela</b> and Pliny, the Sordones held the coastal plain around Ruscino (Château-Roussillon, near Perpignan).</blockquote><a href="/state/sordones">Sordones</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeciras">Algeciras</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=36.13,-5.45&amp;year=43" aria-label="Algeciras on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4413 860 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Pomponius Mela"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4863 1130h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4863 1130h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=36.13,-5.45&amp;year=43">What was here in 43 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
